{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAp3oc5XVvQHa7ujtDmG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I rewrote `scripts/deploy.sh` so every upload carries a Cache-Control header, and wrote up what I did in `ANSWER.md`. I haven't run it against AWS, only syntax-checked it with `bash -n`.\n\n- **HTML, JS and CSS:** `public, max-age=300`, so a changed `booking.js` should reach returning visitors within about five minutes.\n- **`assets/` (png, svg):** `public, max-age=31536000`, which is one year.\n- **Content-Type:** it is set explicitly alongside Cache-Control for each file type. The script stops if `src/` has a file type it has no rule for.\n- **Uploads:** they use `aws s3 cp --recursive` instead of `sync`. `sync` skips unchanged files, so the existing objects with no header would never have been fixed.\n- **Stale files:** a final `sync --delete` removes files no longer in `src/`.\n\nFrom your colleague's note I applied the explicit Content-Type, keeping type and cache policy together in the deploy script, and checking with `curl -sI` rather than trusting a 200. After you deploy, check with `curl -sI https://<site>/booking.js | grep -iE 'content-type|cache-control'`. I didn't use their in-place S3-to-S3 copy, since the deploy re-uploads from `src/`. `ANSWER.md` lists which parts applied.\n\n- **Already-cached copies:** browsers that already cached `booking.js` with no header keep it until it expires. This only fixes future responses.\n- **Logo and mark files:** `logo.png` and `mark.svg` don't change names when they change, so a replaced logo could take a long time to show up under the one-year setting. Rename the file or shorten the TTL if you plan to swap them.\n\nI haven't committed anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1483,"cache_read_input_tokens":24355,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_to